  The blacksmith was gravely wounded, but by some miracle he survived.

  His lonely advance had spurred the shogun's men to action. Unwilling to cede their honor to a peasant, they surged ahead of him like a tidal wave.

  In the end, victory was Yamato's that day. The enemy's assault was savage and relentless, but the brave warriors pushed the Mongols back and held the line until, with all of nature's fury, a typhoon came to sweep them from the sea.

  When it had passed, and they beheld the wreckage of the Mongols' mighty fleet, the men of Yamato fell to their knees and praised every god and buddha they could name. The blacksmith did not begrudge them their worship, but nor did he join in their celebrations.

  In his mind, the gods had been㉗<I>too late.</I> The Divine Wind had not come until the first wave of Mongols had been driven from the shore and hundreds of Yamato's finest lay dead.

  He would not put his faith in such fickle powers. There was too much evil in this world for that.

  Oh yes. He had no illusions. The Mongols, too, had once been men. Only men gave birth to demons. 

  With this new understanding like a fire in his breast, he drove his battle-scarred body back to Kanto. And when he reached the crossroads that led to his village, he turned his back on home without a second thought.

