  And so the blacksmith's will took form. Goro Nyudo Masamune of Soshu. A tsurugi forged to smite evil.

  Though crafted by the hand of no Emishi, this tsurugi was unmatched by any under heaven. All who beheld it were entranced by its beauty, and by the might which that beauty represented.

  To think that a mere Yamatoan could accomplish such craft. Emishi wept tears of despair, while Yamatoan smiths felt hope fill their hearts.

  If Masamune had found a master and gone to battle as he wished, his legend would surely have swept the land. Many Yamatoan smiths, inspired by his example, would have pushed the limits of their art, perhaps enough to surpass the Emishi entire.

  That this did not occur was, as irony would have it, due entirely to Masamune's peerless skill.

  Alas, he had forged㉗<I>too</I> well. Though it graced the armories of daimyo, shoguns, and emperors, not one warrior ever donned his armor; for its majesty was that of the gods, awesome and inviolable. What man would dare to sully it with blood? Such a treasure was meant to be worshipped, all agreed, not worn into battle.

  This was not the glorious crusade he'd imagined. A legend earned on a pedestal meant nothing. He longed for a master who would share in his purpose and wield him against the legions of evil.

  Such a master never came.

  After some time in the keeping of Oda Nobunaga, Masamune vanished from the public eye. He passed in secret from owner to owner, until finally, he was taken to a mountain village and enshrined there to hold a calamity at bay.

  But though his shell lay dormant, there in the darkness, his soul never stopped screaming.

 
 
 
 
 
